sub('Abstract')
para('We implement matrix-free Jacobi iteration for manufactured two- and three-dimensional Poisson problems. A fixed-work study separates runtime scaling from a residual-based convergence and grid-accuracy study. Distributed execution uses Cartesian domain decomposition and blocking face exchange; MPI+OpenMP shares each rank’s local update over its assigned cores. A separate OpenMP target implementation runs on a genuine NVIDIA A40 and retains data on the device between sweeps.')
para('Serial growth is close to the expected quadratic/cubic dependence on side length. The MPI study reaches 288 physical cores and includes strong and weak scaling, matched-core hybrid layouts and rank-level communication/wait diagnostics. A pronounced 144-core slowdown is retained and reviewed rather than removed. GPU results distinguish resident execution from the device data-region end-to-end interval, exposing the significance of overhead at small grids.')
para('Every performance configuration uses one warmup and three retained samples, except additional warmups required on a supplementary allocation. Medians and observed minimum/maximum ranges are reported. We qualify differences in node placement and system conditions and avoid treating fixed 1000-sweep work as universal convergence.')
