para('Here e_d is the unit grid direction. The right-hand side is precomputed. Two buffers ensure that every point reads only the previous sweep; both retain the physical boundary, and the read/write roles alternate after an entire update. The operator is applied without forming a sparse matrix.')
sub('Work, storage and dependencies')
para('A sweep costs Θ(N), K sweeps cost Θ(KN), and storage costs Θ(N). The main arrays occupy approximately 24∏(n_d+2) bytes in double precision, plus communication buffers and runtime overhead. Five/seven stencil neighbors give approximately 5N/7N operator nonzeros, but these are not explicitly stored. The problem size and operator size therefore grow together.')
para('Within a sweep, point updates are independent when the old buffer is fixed. Across subdomains, adjacent old values are needed: the halo stores copies of neighboring ranks’ boundary faces. Physical boundaries are not communication targets, and a face stencil does not require diagonal corner exchange.')
sub('Fixed work versus a converged solve')
para('Performance runs do exactly K=1000 sweeps with no early stop or per-sweep residual reduction. Initial/final diagnostics are outside iteration timing. The numerical solve instead checks R_k=||f-A_hu^k||₂/||f-A_hu^0||₂ every ten sweeps and at the limit. Its main threshold is 1e-8; the accuracy study continues to 1e-10, with a 100000-sweep cap. An initially zero residual is already converged. Solve time includes its periodic checks; fixed-work iteration time does not.')
page('2. Experimental design and validation')
