para('GPU end-to-end means the solver device-data-region interval, not complete command walltime. Resident is not a pure device-event kernel-only measurement. Both GPU intervals come from one invocation; differences include several operations and cannot isolate PCIe transfer time.')
page('11. CPU/GPU runtime results')
fig('gpu_runtime','Vanda A40 and its matched CPU baselines, K=1000. Medians of three formal runs; bars are actual min/max, warmups excluded. Both axes are logarithmic. CPU1/CPU12 and GPU run within the same allocation.',maxheight=104*mm)
table(['Grid','CPU1 s','CPU12 s','Resident s','End-to-end s'],[[f'{n}³']+[fmt(ct(n,bk,t,m)['median'],6) for bk,t,m in [('cpu',1,'cpu_seconds'),('cpu',12,'cpu_seconds'),('gpu',1,'gpu_resident_seconds'),('gpu',1,'gpu_e2e_seconds')]] for n in [64,128,256]],[22*mm,38*mm,38*mm,38*mm,38*mm])
para('CPU12 speedups at 64³/128³/256³ are 6.452/7.293/10.757, with efficiencies 53.77%/60.77%/89.64%. This compares only one and twelve threads; it does not establish a full CPU thread-scaling curve or an optimum thread count.')
para('CPU12 at 128³ ranges from 0.572921 to 0.870379 s; all samples remain. The large-grid residuals after 1000 sweeps are approximately 0.0047745, 0.00626234 and 0.00666474, so the timings do not represent equal converged accuracy. Small-grid full-array identity is not extrapolated to every large performance grid.')
page('12. GPU speedup and overhead interpretation')
fig('gpu_speedup','Ratios of medians, with CPU1 (left) or CPU12 (right) as numerator. Above one means GPU is faster. Size axes are logarithmic; speedup axes are linear with separate scales. No paired confidence interval is implied.',maxheight=88*mm)
table(['Grid','CPU1 / resident','CPU1 / E2E','CPU12 / resident','CPU12 / E2E'],[[r['n']+'³',fmt(r['gpu_resident_vs_cpu1']),fmt(r['gpu_e2e_vs_cpu1']),fmt(r['gpu_resident_vs_cpuTc']),fmt(r['gpu_e2e_vs_cpuTc'])] for r in rat],[22*mm,38*mm,38*mm,38*mm,38*mm])
para('The table labels name the GPU scope and CPU baseline; entries are CPU-time/GPU-time, not the inverse. At 64³, resident GPU is faster than CPU12 but end-to-end GPU is slower (0.795×). At 128³ and 256³, end-to-end speedups against CPU12 are 2.817× and 2.688×. The exact crossover size was not measured.')
para('Resident speedup against CPU12 is not monotonic: 2.338×, 4.249×, 2.938×. The difference of end-to-end and resident medians is about 0.1031, 0.1039 and 0.1517 s, approximately 66.0%, 33.7% and 8.5% of the end-to-end medians. Relative data-region overhead falls with size, but that difference aggregates allocation, mapping, transfers and release. GPU efficiency is not defined by treating an accelerator as a CPU core count.')
page('13. Overall design discussion and limitations')
para('MPI expresses distributed ownership and halo dependencies, OpenMP shares a rank’s CPU work, and target offload explicitly maps arrays to a different device memory space. They address different costs: inter-rank face messages, thread synchronization and shared-memory locality, and host/device movement plus kernel launch. Keeping device data resident removes repeated transfers without removing synchronization or launch overhead.')
para('Strong scaling reduces work per rank until communication/wait and synchronization become relatively expensive. Weak scaling holds local updates fixed but cannot hold network state, neighbor structure and memory contention fixed. The measured 144-core waiting anomaly is reported with an overlapping-node repeat and rank-phase evidence rather than assigned a speculative root cause. A synthetic MMS problem supports correctness and complexity claims; it does not support conclusions about a real dataset.')
para('A hybrid layout changes rank count and thread count together. Equal-total-core comparisons use paired pure-MPI baselines in the same allocation. A benefit or loss therefore cannot be attributed solely to OpenMP without further profiling. CPU/GPU comparisons similarly stay within C’s allocation and compiler stack; a B CPU time is not divided by a C GPU time and presented as a controlled speedup.')
sub('Boundaries of the evidence')
table(['Limitation','Consequence'],[['Three formal samples','Min/max show observed variability, not confidence bounds or guaranteed ordering'],['Shared cluster and node transitions','Some differences can reflect scheduling, fabric/topology or shared memory-system conditions'],['Memory family / hybrid cohort','The primary scaling cohort and paired hybrid cohorts are labelled separately; unlike machines are not silently pooled'],['Instrumented phase samples','One run per point, timers add cost; blocking wait is not pure network transfer'],['Fixed K','Residual accuracy differs with grid size; numerical-study convergence is separate'],['C scheduler provenance','Supplied samples/device/validation/status checked; independent final PBS Exit_status not available'],['Optional high-core queue','432/720-core points are omitted until successful execution; >=256-core evidence uses actual 288-core runs']],[48*mm,126*mm])
page('14. Reproduction and AI assistance')
sub('Build and representative correctness checks')
para('A numerical route: make numerics. Fixed-size scaling uses a_scaling_20261104/jacobi_a_scaling.cpp with GCC/C++17/-O2 and the supplied PBS/runner. B route: source b_mpi/runtime_modules.sh on a Vanda compute node and run make b-mpi; verify_b.py checks the representative cases. C CPU/GPU route: bash c_openmp/build.sh nvidia under the supplied NVHPC module configuration, then use run_c.py and make_job.py. Compilation and numerical work run on compute allocations, not login nodes.')
para('The source package README gives exact representative arguments and plotting commands. The concise CSV inputs regenerate every report plot using closeout/plot_report.py. Raw command output, stderr, qstat, executables and full job archives are internal evidence and are excluded from the course source package. The original B cohort is retained internally; the matched-memory scaling cohort and the 144 repeat with shared hosts are explicitly selected rather than pooled.')
sub('AI assistance')
para('ChatGPT/Codex assisted with implementation, independent checks, experiment scripts, cluster execution after the user authenticated, numerical auditing, plotting and English drafting. Tool execution and generated output do not establish a member’s personal understanding or authorship. Human members remain responsible for reviewing the mathematics, code, findings and final course submission. Names, student IDs and personal contribution statements are intentionally not supplied in this review edition at the user’s request.')
sub('Evidence and references')
para('The mathematical derivations are given in Sections 1–2. Numerical inputs are the supplied A convergence/serial summaries, B per-run audited records and cohort summaries, and C’s formal samples/environment/validation summaries. The course project specification is transcribed in docs/PROJECT_REQUIREMENTS.md. The source package contains the actual implementations and reproduction scripts; no unavailable lecture slide or scheduler record is claimed to have been independently inspected.')
para('Before course upload, members must add their identification and actual contribution/AI declarations, review their assigned code and explanations, and confirm the Canvas deadline. This work prepares the technical review PDF and a source/figure/CSV whitelist; it does not upload to Canvas or send messages on behalf of members.')

